# Valgrind leak suppressions for Dockerfile.valgrind
#
# The Ubuntu 24.04 image ships bash 5.2.  When ctest -T memcheck wraps each
# test script under valgrind, every bash subshell that runs (the test
# runner itself and every tool it spawns) leaks a small fixed number of
# bytes from its own internal data structures: I/O redirection state from
# redirection_expand(), argv arrays from strvec_from_word_list(), and a
# handful of other word-list and execute_command_internal sites.
#
# We verified that TBOL binaries themselves are clean by running valgrind
# directly on each tool with a representative input:
#
#   tbolc, tboldc, tboldasm, tbolfmt    -> definitely lost: 0 bytes
#   test-lsp (16,510 allocs, 16,506 frees, 1.6 MB total) -> 0 bytes lost
#
# Bash never calls into TBOL code, so suppressing any definite leak whose
# top frame is malloc() called from /usr/bin/bash cannot mask a real leak
# in TBOL.  Any leak rooted in a TBOL binary will still be reported.

{
   bash-internal-allocation
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: definite
   fun:malloc
   obj:/usr/bin/bash
}
